            <ArticleTitle>Functional use of secondary cancer symptomatology.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>267-75</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This article presents several case studies which illustrate the way in which a terminal patient's attention may be diverted from the primary disease process, cancer, to secondary somatic symptomatology such as pain and nausea. This phenomenon has important consequences for the patient, family, physician, and the patient's medical treatment. These consequences are discussed in terms of primary gain, tertiary gain, and the &quot;medicalization of existential problems.&quot;</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
